WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.322
A complete aggregation query has a natural logical flow.

00:00:04.322 --> 00:00:07.537
First, FROM identifies the source.

00:00:07.537 --> 00:00:10.102
WHERE filters individual rows. GROUP BY forms the groups.

00:00:10.102 --> 00:00:16.382
HAVING removes groups that do not meet an aggregate condition.

00:00:16.382 --> 00:00:20.167
SELECT defines the values that appear in the result, and ORDER BY sorts the final rows.

00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:29.322
The written SQL order is familiar, but thinking about the logical stages helps explain why certain expressions

00:00:29.322 --> 00:00:32.652
are valid in one clause and not another.

00:00:32.652 --> 00:00:36.072
There is also a practical efficiency lesson here.

00:00:36.072 --> 00:00:43.067
If a condition can be applied to individual source rows, put it in WHERE so fewer rows reach

00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:43.432
the grouping stage.

00:00:43.432 --> 00:00:50.302
Before running a long summary query, read it as a pipeline: source rows, row filter, groups, group filter,

00:00:50.302 --> 00:00:57.822
output, sort. When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:57.822 --> 00:01:02.247
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:01:02.247 --> 00:01:06.592
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:06.592 --> 00:01:12.192
In a real setting such as a student-success report comparing course-level outcomes,

00:01:12.192 --> 00:01:18.417
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:18.417 --> 00:01:20.807
before discussing syntax.

00:01:20.807 --> 00:01:27.171
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
